BT /F1 9 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 758 Tm (RELATIVITY: THE SPECIAL AND GENERAL THEORY) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 738 Tm (PART I: THE SPECIAL THEORY OF RELATIVITY) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 713 Tm (I. Physical Meaning of Geometrical Propositions) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 688 Tm (In your schooldays most of you who read this book made acquaintance with the noble building) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 675.5 Tm (of Euclid's geometry, and you remember--perhaps with more respect than love--the magnificent) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 663 Tm (structure, on the lofty staircase of which you were chased about for uncounted hours by) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 650.5 Tm (conscientious teachers. By reason of our past experience, you would certainly regard) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 638 Tm (everyone with disdain who should pronounce even the most out-of-the-way proposition of this) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 625.5 Tm (science to be untrue. But perhaps this feeling of proud certainty would leave you) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 613 Tm (immediately if some one were to ask you: "What, then, do you mean by the assertion that) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 600.5 Tm (these propositions are true?" Let us proceed to give this question a little consideration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 575.5 Tm (Geometry sets out from certain conceptions such as "plane," "point," and "straight line,") Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 563 Tm (with which we are able to associate more or less definite ideas, and from certain simple) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 550.5 Tm (propositions \(axioms\) which, in virtue of these ideas, we are inclined to accept as "true.") Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 538 Tm (Then, on the basis of a logical process, the justification of which we feel ourselves) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 525.5 Tm (compelled to admit, all remaining propositions are shown to follow from those axioms, i.e.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 513 Tm (they are proven. A proposition is then correct \("true"\) when it has been derived in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 500.5 Tm (recognised manner from the axioms. The question of "truth" of the individual geometrical) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 488 Tm (propositions is thus reduced to one of the "truth" of the axioms. Now it has long been known) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 475.5 Tm (that the last question is not only unanswerable by the methods of geometry, but that it is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 463 Tm (in itself entirely without meaning. We cannot ask whether it is true that only one straight) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 450.5 Tm (line goes through two points. We can only say that Euclidean geometry deals with things) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 438 Tm (called "straight lines," to each of which is ascribed the property of being uniquely) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 425.5 Tm (determined by two points situated on it. The concept "true" does not tally with the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 413 Tm (assertions of pure geometry, because by the word "true" we are eventually in the habit of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 400.5 Tm (designating always the correspondence with a "real" object; geometry, however, is not) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 388 Tm (concerned with the relation of the ideas involved in it to objects of experience, but only) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 375.5 Tm (with the logical connection of these ideas among themselves.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 350.5 Tm (It is not difficult to understand why, in spite of this, we feel constrained to call the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 338 Tm (propositions of geometry "true." Geometrical ideas correspond to more or less exact objects) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 325.5 Tm (in nature, and these last are undoubtedly the exclusive cause of the genesis of those ideas.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 313 Tm (Geometry ought to refrain from such a course, in order to give to its structure the largest) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 300.5 Tm (possible logical unity. The practice, for example, of seeing in a "distance" two marked) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 288 Tm (positions on a practically rigid body is something which is lodged deeply in our habit of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 275.5 Tm (thought. We are accustomed further to regard three points as being situated on a straight) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 263 Tm (line, if their apparent positions can be made to coincide for observation with one eye,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 250.5 Tm (under suitable choice of our place of observation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 225.5 Tm (If, in pursuance of our habit of thought, we now supplement the propositions of Euclidean) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 213 Tm (geometry by the single proposition that two points on a practically rigid body always) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 200.5 Tm (correspond to the same distance \(line-interval\), independently of any changes in position to) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 188 Tm (which we may subject the body, the propositions of Euclidean geometry then resolve) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 175.5 Tm (themselves into propositions on the possible relative position of practically rigid bodies.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 163 Tm (Geometry which has been supplemented in this way is then to be treated as a branch of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 150.5 Tm (physics. We can now legitimately ask as to the "truth" of geometrical propositions) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 138 Tm (interpreted in this way, since we are justified in asking whether these propositions are) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 125.5 Tm (satisfied for those real things we have associated with the geometrical ideas. In less exact) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 113 Tm (terms we can express this by saying that by the "truth" of a geometrical proposition in this) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 100.5 Tm (sense we understand its validity for a construction with rule and compasses.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 1 0 0 1 295 40 Tm (- 1 -) Tj ET
