BT /F1 9 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 758 Tm (RELATIVITY: THE SPECIAL AND GENERAL THEORY) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 738 Tm (the physics of measurement this is attained by the application of the Cartesian system of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 725.5 Tm (co-ordinates.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 700.5 Tm (This consists of three plane surfaces perpendicular to each other and rigidly attached to a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 688 Tm (rigid body. Referred to a system of co-ordinates, the scene of any event will be determined) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 675.5 Tm (\(for the main part\) by the specification of the lengths of the three perpendiculars or) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 663 Tm (co-ordinates \(x, y, z\) which can be dropped from the scene of the event to those three plane) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 650.5 Tm (surfaces. The lengths of these three perpendiculars can be determined by a series of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 638 Tm (manipulations with rigid measuring-rods performed according to the rules and methods laid) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 625.5 Tm (down by Euclidean geometry.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 600.5 Tm (In practice, the rigid surfaces which constitute the system of co-ordinates are generally) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 588 Tm (not available; furthermore, the magnitudes of the co-ordinates are not actually determined) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 575.5 Tm (by constructions with rigid rods, but by indirect means. If the results of physics and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 563 Tm (astronomy are to maintain their clearness, the physical meaning of specifications of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 550.5 Tm (position must always be sought in accordance with the above considerations.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 525.5 Tm (We thus obtain the following result: Every description of events in space involves the use) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 513 Tm (of a rigid body to which such events have to be referred. The resulting relationship takes) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 500.5 Tm (for granted that the laws of Euclidean geometry hold for "distances;" the "distance" being) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 488 Tm (represented physically by means of the convention of two marks on a rigid body.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 463 Tm (III. Space and Time in Classical Mechanics) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 438 Tm (The purpose of mechanics is to describe how bodies change their position in space with) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 425.5 Tm ("time." I should load my conscience with grave sins against the sacred spirit of lucidity) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 413 Tm (were I to formulate the aims of mechanics in this way, without serious reflection and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 400.5 Tm (detailed explanations. Let us proceed to disclose these sins.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 375.5 Tm (It is not clear what is to be understood here by "position" and "space." I stand at the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 363 Tm (window of a railway carriage which is travelling uniformly, and drop a stone on the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 350.5 Tm (embankment, without throwing it. Then, disregarding the influence of the air resistance, I) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 338 Tm (see the stone descend in a straight line. A pedestrian who observes the misdeed from the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 325.5 Tm (footpath notices that the stone falls to earth in a parabolic curve. I now ask: Do the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 313 Tm ("positions" traversed by the stone lie "in reality" on a straight line or on a parabola?) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 300.5 Tm (Moreover, what is meant here by motion "in space"? From the considerations of the previous) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 288 Tm (section the answer is self-evident. In the first place we entirely shun the vague word) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 275.5 Tm ("space," of which, we must honestly acknowledge, we cannot form the slightest conception,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 263 Tm (and we replace it by "motion relative to a practically rigid body of reference." The) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 250.5 Tm (positions relative to the body of reference \(railway carriage or embankment\) have already) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 238 Tm (been defined in detail in the preceding section. If instead of "body of reference" we insert) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 225.5 Tm ("system of co-ordinates," which is a useful idea for mathematical description, we are in a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 213 Tm (position to say: The stone traverses a straight line relative to a system of co-ordinates) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 200.5 Tm (rigidly attached to the carriage, but relative to a system of co-ordinates rigidly attached) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 188 Tm (to the ground \(embankment\) it describes a parabola. With the aid of this example it is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 175.5 Tm (clearly seen that there is no such thing as an independently existing trajectory \(lit.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 163 Tm ("path-curve", but only a trajectory relative to a particular body of reference.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 138 Tm (In order to have a complete description of the motion, we must specify how the body alters) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 125.5 Tm (its position with time; i.e. for every point on the trajectory it must be stated at what) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 113 Tm (time the body is situated there. These data must be supplemented by such a definition of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 100.5 Tm (time that, in virtue of this definition, these time-values can be regarded essentially as) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 88 Tm (magnitudes \(results of measurements\) capable of observation. If we take our stand on the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 1 0 0 1 295 40 Tm (- 3 -) Tj ET
