BT /F1 9 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 758 Tm (RELATIVITY: THE SPECIAL AND GENERAL THEORY) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 738 Tm (ground of classical mechanics, we can satisfy this requirement for our illustration in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 725.5 Tm (following manner. We imagine two clocks of identical construction; the man at the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 713 Tm (railway-carriage window is holding one of them, and the man on the footpath the other. Each) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 700.5 Tm (of the observers determines the position on his own reference-body occupied by the stone at) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 688 Tm (each tick of the clock he is holding in his hand. In this connection we have not taken) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 675.5 Tm (account of the inaccuracy involved by the finiteness of the velocity of propagation of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 663 Tm (light. With this and with a second difficulty prevailing here we shall have to deal in) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 650.5 Tm (detail later.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 625.5 Tm (IV. THE GALILEIAN SYSTEM OF CO-ORDINATES) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 600.5 Tm (As is well known, the fundamental law of the mechanics of Galilei-Newton, which is known as) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 588 Tm (the law of inertia, can be stated thus: A body removed sufficiently far from other bodies) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 575.5 Tm (continues in a state of rest or of uniform motion in a straight line. This law not only says) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 563 Tm (something about the motion of the bodies, but it also indicates the reference-bodies or) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 550.5 Tm (systems of coordinates, permissible in mechanics, which can be used in mechanical) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 538 Tm (description. The visible fixed stars are bodies for which the law of inertia certainly holds) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 525.5 Tm (to a high degree of approximation. Now if we use a system of co-ordinates which is rigidly) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 513 Tm (attached to the earth, then, relative to this system, every fixed star describes a circle of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 500.5 Tm (immense radius in the course of an astronomical day, a result which is opposed to the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 488 Tm (statement of the law of inertia. So that if we adhere to this law we must refer these) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 475.5 Tm (motions only to systems of coordinates relative to which the fixed stars do not move in a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 463 Tm (circle. A system of co-ordinates of which the state of motion is such that the law of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 450.5 Tm (inertia holds relative to it is called a "Galileian system of co-ordinates." The laws of the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 438 Tm (mechanics of Galilei-Newton can be regarded as valid only for a Galileian system of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 425.5 Tm (co-ordinates.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 400.5 Tm (V. The Principle of Relativity \(in the Restricted Sense\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 375.5 Tm (In order to attain the greatest possible clearness, let us return to our example of the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 363 Tm (railway carriage supposed to be travelling uniformly. We call its motion a uniform) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 350.5 Tm (translation \("uniform" because it is of constant velocity and direction, "translation") Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 338 Tm (because although the carriage changes its position relative to the embankment yet it does) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 325.5 Tm (not rotate in so doing\). Let us imagine a raven flying through the air in such a manner that) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 313 Tm (its motion, as observed from the embankment, is uniform and in a straight line. If we were) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 300.5 Tm (to observe the flying raven from the moving railway carriage. we should find that the motion) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 288 Tm (of the raven would be one of different velocity and direction, but that it would still be) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 275.5 Tm (uniform and in a straight line. Expressed in an abstract manner we may say: If a mass m is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 263 Tm (moving uniformly in a straight line with respect to a co-ordinate system K, then it will) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 250.5 Tm (also be moving uniformly and in a straight line relative to a second co-ordinate system K') Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 238 Tm (provided that the latter is executing a uniform translatory motion with respect to K. In) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 225.5 Tm (accordance with the discussion contained in the preceding section, it follows that:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 200.5 Tm (If K is a Galileian co-ordinate system. then every other co-ordinate system K' is a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 188 Tm (Galileian one, when, in relation to K, it is in a condition of uniform motion of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 175.5 Tm (translation. Relative to K' the mechanical laws of Galilei-Newton hold good exactly as they) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 163 Tm (do with respect to K.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 138 Tm (We advance a step farther in our generalisation when we express the tenet thus: If, relative) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 125.5 Tm (to K, K' is a uniformly moving co-ordinate system devoid of rotation, then natural phenomena) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 113 Tm (run their course with respect to K' according to exactly the same general laws as with) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 100.5 Tm (respect to K. This statement is called the principle of relativity \(in the restricted) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 88 Tm (sense\).) Tj ET
