BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 713 Tm (with respect to the embankment. As a natural consequence, however, the following question) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 700.5 Tm (arises:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 675.5 Tm (Are two events \(e.g. the two strokes of lightning A and B\) which are simultaneous with) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 663 Tm (reference to the railway embankment also simultaneous relatively to the train? We shall show) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 650.5 Tm (directly that the answer must be in the negative.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 625.5 Tm (When we say that the lightning strokes A and B are simultaneous with respect to be) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 613 Tm (embankment, we mean: the rays of light emitted at the places A and B, where the lightning) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 600.5 Tm (occurs, meet each other at the mid-point M of the length A to B of the embankment. But the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 588 Tm (events A and B also correspond to positions A and B on the train. Let M' be the mid-point of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 575.5 Tm (the distance A to B on the travelling train. Just when the flashes \(as judged from the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 563 Tm (embankment\) of lightning occur, this point M' naturally coincides with the point M but it) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 550.5 Tm (moves towards the right in the diagram with the velocity v of the train. If an observer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 538 Tm (sitting in the position M' in the train did not possess this velocity, then he would remain) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 525.5 Tm (permanently at M, and the light rays emitted by the flashes of lightning A and B would reach) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 513 Tm (him simultaneously, i.e. they would meet just where he is situated. Now in reality) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 500.5 Tm (\(considered with reference to the railway embankment\) he is hastening towards the beam of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 488 Tm (light coming from B, whilst he is riding on ahead of the beam of light coming from A. Hence) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 475.5 Tm (the observer will see the beam of light emitted from B earlier than he will see that emitted) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 463 Tm (from A. Observers who take the railway train as their reference-body must therefore come to) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 450.5 Tm (the conclusion that the lightning flash B took place earlier than the lightning flash A. We) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 438 Tm (thus arrive at the important result:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 413 Tm (Events which are simultaneous with reference to the embankment are not simultaneous with) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 400.5 Tm (respect to the train, and vice versa \(relativity of simultaneity\). Every reference-body) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 388 Tm (\(co-ordinate system\) has its own particular time; unless we are told the reference-body to) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 375.5 Tm (which the statement of time refers, there is no meaning in a statement of the time of an) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 363 Tm (event.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 338 Tm (Now before the advent of the theory of relativity it had always tacitly been assumed in) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 325.5 Tm (physics that the statement of time had an absolute significance, i.e. that it is independent) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 313 Tm (of the state of motion of the body of reference. But we have just seen that this assumption) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 300.5 Tm (is incompatible with the most natural definition of simultaneity; if we discard this) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 288 Tm (assumption, then the conflict between the law of the propagation of light in vacuo and the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 275.5 Tm (principle of relativity \(developed in Section VII\) disappears.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 250.5 Tm (We were led to that conflict by the considerations of Section VI, which are now no longer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 238 Tm (tenable. In that section we concluded that the man in the carriage, who traverses the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 225.5 Tm (distance w per second relative to the carriage, traverses the same distance also with) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 213 Tm (respect to the embankment in each second of time. But, according to the foregoing) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 200.5 Tm (considerations, the time required by a particular occurrence with respect to the carriage) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 188 Tm (must not be considered equal to the duration of the same occurrence as judged from the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 175.5 Tm (embankment \(as reference-body\). Hence it cannot be contended that the man in walking travels) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 163 Tm (the distance w relative to the railway line in a time which is equal to one second as judged) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 150.5 Tm (from the embankment.) Tj ET
