BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 713 Tm (XV. General Results of the Theory) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 688 Tm (It is clear from our previous considerations that the \(special\) theory of relativity has) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 675.5 Tm (grown out of electrodynamics and optics. In these fields it has not appreciably altered the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 663 Tm (predictions of theory, but it has considerably simplified the theoretical structure, i.e.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 650.5 Tm (the derivation of laws, and--what is incomparably more important--it has considerably) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 638 Tm (reduced the number of independent hypotheses forming the basis of theory. The special theory) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 625.5 Tm (of relativity has rendered the Maxwell-Lorentz theory so plausible, that the latter would) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 613 Tm (have been generally accepted by physicists even if experiment had decided less unequivocally) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 600.5 Tm (in its favour.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 575.5 Tm (Classical mechanics required to be modified before it could come into line with the demands) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 563 Tm (of the special theory of relativity. For the main part, however, this modification affects) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 550.5 Tm (only the laws for rapid motions, in which the velocities of matter v are not very small as) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 538 Tm (compared with the velocity of light. We have experience of such rapid motions only in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 525.5 Tm (case of electrons and ions; for other motions the variations from the laws of classical) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 513 Tm (mechanics are too small to make themselves evident in practice. We shall not consider the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 500.5 Tm (motion of stars until we come to speak of the general theory of relativity. In accordance) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 488 Tm (with the theory of relativity the kinetic energy of a material point of mass m is no longer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 475.5 Tm (given by the well-known expression) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 450.5 Tm (but by the expression) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 425.5 Tm (This expression approaches infinity as the velocity v approaches the velocity of light c.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 413 Tm (The velocity must therefore always remain less than c, however great may be the energies) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 400.5 Tm (used to produce the acceleration. If we develop the expression for the kinetic energy in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 388 Tm (form of a series, we obtain) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 363 Tm (is small compared with unity, the third of these terms is always small in comparison with) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 350.5 Tm (the second, which last is alone considered in classical mechanics. The first term mc2 does) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 338 Tm (not contain the velocity, and requires no consideration if we are only dealing with the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 325.5 Tm (question as to how the energy of a point-mass; depends on the velocity. We shall speak of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 313 Tm (its essential significance later.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 288 Tm (The most important result of a general character to which the special theory of relativity) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 275.5 Tm (has led is concerned with the conception of mass. Before the advent of relativity, physics) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 263 Tm (recognised two conservation laws of fundamental importance, namely, the law of the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 250.5 Tm (conservation of energy and the law of the conservation of mass these two fundamental laws) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 238 Tm (appeared to be quite independent of each other. By means of the theory of relativity they) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 225.5 Tm (have been united into one law. We shall now briefly consider how this unification came) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 213 Tm (about, and what meaning is to be attached to it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 188 Tm (The principle of relativity requires that the law of the conservation of energy should hold) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 175.5 Tm (not only with reference to a co-ordinate system K, but also with respect to every) Tj ET
