BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 588 Tm (In one of the most notable of these attempts Michelson devised a method which appears as) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 575.5 Tm (though it must be decisive. Imagine two mirrors so arranged on a rigid body that the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 563 Tm (reflecting surfaces face each other. A ray of light requires a perfectly definite time T to) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 550.5 Tm (pass from one mirror to the other and back again, if the whole system be at rest with) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 538 Tm (respect to the ther. It is found by calculation, however, that a slightly different time T') Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 525.5 Tm (is required for this process, if the body, together with the mirrors, be moving relatively) Tj ET
